PRD — Emergency Response App: Frontend

Owner: Jeyakrishnan Scope: frontend/ only (reporter.html, dashboard.html, style.css, script.js) — lives in a shared repo alongside the teammate's backend Status: Neither page has been built yet — both are starting from scratch Backend dependency: FastAPI on Vercel serverless functions, same origin as the static frontend under /api/ (see BACKEND_PLAN.md). No separate host, no CORS. Live updates via Supabase Realtime, not a raw WebSocket.

Build-ready. Both pages are being built from scratch. Every integration detail below is derived directly from the actual backend plan, with no assumed endpoints, statuses, or fields that don't exist there. All prior open questions are resolved as concrete decisions in Section 10. Mock mode is required everywhere so this can be built and demoed with no backend running.

1. Problem & context

This is a course project simulating an emergency dispatch system for the Chennai area — not connected to real government infrastructure. The frontend has two distinct users who never see each other's screen:

The reporter — someone in distress, using a phone, possibly panicked. Needs one obvious action, minimal reading, fast confirmation that help is coming.
The station operator — staff at a dispatch station, using a larger screen. Needs to scan multiple incoming alerts fast, judge severity at a glance, and see where each alert is on a map.

The two pages share a visual language (dark, functional, "control room" aesthetic) but are optimized for opposite conditions: one for stress and speed, one for scanning and situational awareness.

2. Goals
A reporter can raise an alert in under 10 seconds of interaction, without reading instructions.
A station operator can tell alert severity and location at a glance, without opening each alert individually.
Both pages work convincingly as a demo even before the backend is deployed (mock-mode fallback everywhere).
Frontend is deployable as static files on Vercel, calling the same-origin /api/ functions once merged into the shared repo.
3. Non-goals
No user accounts, login, or auth — this is a public-access emergency flow, not a logged-in product.
No native mobile app — mobile web only, via browser.
No offline support / PWA caching in this phase.
No admin tools for managing the station directory (that's the backend's seed data job).
No backend code, Supabase schema, or Gemini prompt work — that's the teammate's scope.
