5. Page 1 — Reporter (reporter.html) — 🔲 Not started
Core flow
Page loads → connectivity check is a lightweight GET /api/stations call (the backend has no /health endpoint; /api/stations is cheap, read-only, and always available), shown as a small connection-status indicator.
User presses and holds the SOS button.
On press: geolocation request fires, speech recognition starts, live transcript renders. If SpeechRecognition is unsupported, the button is replaced by a text input + submit (see FR4).
On release: recognition stops, payload POSTs to POST /api/alert:
json
   { "transcript": "...", "latitude": 13.05, "longitude": 80.21 }
Response renders a dispatch card from:
json
   { "alert": { "id": "...", "type": "...", "severity": 5, "summary": "...", "status": "dispatched", "created_at": "..." },
     "station": { "id": "...", "name": "...", "latitude": 13.048, "longitude": 80.208, "distance_km": 1.4 } }

Use alert.id as the reference ID. The card shows an estimated ETA computed client-side as round(distance_km / 40 * 60) minutes (40 km/h assumed average emergency-response urban speed), labeled "Estimated ETA" — never presented as a backend-calculated figure. 6. While the alert is active (status not resolved), the page calls POST /api/alerts/:id/checkin with { "motion_ok": true|false } every 30 seconds. Response { alert, escalated } — if escalated is true, update the dispatch card's severity/status in place and show a brief "situation escalated" notice.

Functional requirements
FR1: Press-and-hold interaction (mouse + touch), not tap-to-toggle — reduces accidental triggers.
FR2: Location and microphone permission requests run in parallel and fail independently — a denied permission on one doesn't block the other.
FR3: Live transcript displayed word-by-word as the user speaks (interim + final results).
FR4: If SpeechRecognition isn't supported (Firefox, Safari), swap the press-and-hold button for a textarea + "Send alert" button that submits the same POST /api/alert payload (transcript typed instead of spoken). No dead end for unsupported browsers.
FR5: Mock mode triggers automatically whenever POST /api/alert fails or times out (no live backend reachable) — simulates a dispatch response so the UI stays demoable. No config constant to toggle.
FR6: Failed submission (after mock-mode fallback also fails, e.g. JS error) shows an explicit error and a suggestion to call emergency services directly — never fails silently.
FR7: 30-second check-in loop per Core flow step 6; a failed check-in retries once, then silently backs off — it must never crash or block the reporter's view of their dispatch card.
Non-functional requirements
Usable one-handed on a phone screen down to ~360px width.
Visible focus states for keyboard/switch-access users.
Respects prefers-reduced-motion (ambient ring pulse disabled).
No blocking modals or multi-step forms in the critical path.
Out of scope for this page
Multi-language voice input (currently en-IN only).
6. Page 2 — Dashboard (dashboard.html) — 🔲 Not started
Core flow
Page loads → GET /api/alerts and GET /api/stations for initial state, then subscribes to a Supabase Realtime channel on the alerts table (postgres_changes, event *) for live inserts/updates — no WebSocket, no polling after the initial load. If the Realtime subscription can't connect (e.g. no config/credentials available yet), fall into mock mode: simulate new alerts appearing on an interval so the page stays demoable.
Incoming/changed alerts update the list, tagged with severity and an age indicator derived from created_at.
Map (Leaflet.js/OpenStreetMap — see Section 6a) plots each alert's location alongside station positions from GET /api/stations.
Operator selects an alert to see full detail (GET /api/alerts/:id) and change its status via PATCH /api/alerts/:id.
On a new alert with severity === 5, play a single short audio tone (one cue, not a looping siren) alongside the visual indicator — critical alerts must not rely on the operator looking at the screen at the right moment.
When status becomes resolved, the alert moves out of the main feed into a collapsed "Resolved" section rather than disappearing, so a demo can show full history on request.
Functional requirements
FR1: Real-time alert feed via a Supabase Realtime channel subscription on the alerts table.
FR2: Severity encoded visually (color + label), not color alone — for colorblind accessibility.
FR3: Map view shows all active alerts as markers; clicking a marker highlights the corresponding list row and vice versa.
FR4: Each alert card shows: summary, time since created_at, distance_km from station, severity, status.
FR5: Operator can update alert status via PATCH /api/alerts/:id with { "status": "..." }, using only the backend's real enum: reported | dispatched | monitoring | resolved | escalated. The operator-facing "Acknowledge" button sets status to monitoring (the closest existing value — there is no separate acknowledged status in this backend).
FR6: Connection-loss state (Realtime subscription dropped) is visually distinct from "no alerts" — an empty feed and a dead subscription must never look the same.
FR7: Single audio cue on new critical (severity 5) alerts, per Core flow step 5. No audio for lower severities.
FR8: Resolved alerts collapse into a separate section rather than vanishing, per Core flow step 6.
Render unrecognized type/status/category values sensibly rather than breaking — both documents note these enums are expected to grow.
6a. Map integration (Leaflet + OpenStreetMap)

Client-side only — this is not a backend integration point, needs no API key, and requires no endpoint from the teammate's backend beyond the lat/lng fields already present in GET /api/alerts and GET /api/stations.

Load via CDN in dashboard.html:

html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

Initialize the map, then add the tile layer after the map object exists:

js
const map = L.map('map').setView([13.05, 80.21], 12); // center on Chennai

L.tileLayer('https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
    maxZoom: 19,
    attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap contributors</a>'
}).addTo(map);
maxZoom: 19 avoids blurry tiles when zoomed close on a station/alert marker.
Attribution is required by OSM's usage policy — keep it visible, don't remove or hide it.
Only one tile layer should ever be added to the map — if scaffolding already includes a placeholder tile layer, replace it rather than stacking a second one.
Station markers (from GET /api/stations) and alert markers (from GET /api/alerts/Realtime) use separate marker icons/colors so they're visually distinguishable at a glance (ties into FR2's severity-by-color-and-label requirement).
Non-functional requirements
Must remain legible and usable with 10+ simultaneous alerts on screen.
Designed for a stable desktop viewport first; mobile support is a nice-to-have, not a requirement.
Out of scope for this phase
Multi-station coordination (handing an alert from one station to another).
Historical/resolved-alert archive view beyond the collapsed section in this session.
Operator authentication.
