8. Technical integration points
Frontend calls	Method/shape
GET /api/stations	Used for map markers and as the reporter page's connectivity check
POST /api/alert	Body: { transcript, latitude, longitude } → Returns { alert: {...}, station: {...} }
GET /api/alerts	Optional ?status= filter → Returns { alerts: [...] }
GET /api/alerts/:id	Returns a single alert's full detail
PATCH /api/alerts/:id	Body: { status } (one of reported|dispatched|monitoring|resolved|escalated) → Returns the updated alert object
POST /api/alerts/:id/checkin	Body: { motion_ok } → Returns { alert: {...}, escalated: boolean }
Supabase Realtime	Dashboard subscribes to postgres_changes on the alerts table; URL + anon key provided once the project is set up (safe to expose client-side)
OpenStreetMap tiles (tile.openstreetmap.org)	Client-side only — no backend endpoint, no API key; see Section 6a

All backend routes are same-origin (/api/... on the same Vercel deployment as the static frontend, once merged into the shared repo) — no BACKEND_URL constant, no CORS handling. No authentication in v1.

Code defensively: fields may be added, enum lists may grow, and requests can be slow/flaky (serverless + prototype) — handle timeouts and partial data without breaking the page.

9. Success criteria for this phase
Reporter flow completable by a first-time user with zero instructions, in one take, on any major browser (voice on Chrome, text fallback elsewhere).
Dashboard operator can correctly identify the most severe active alert within 3 seconds of it appearing, in a demo with 5+ simulated concurrent alerts.
Both pages run standalone in mock mode for grading/demo purposes even if the backend is down or not yet merged into the shared repo.
10. Decisions made in this revision
Audio on critical alerts: yes, one short tone per new severity-5 alert. Chosen because the dashboard is meant to be scanned continuously across a shift; a purely visual cue risks being missed, and a single tone (not a looping siren) avoids alarm fatigue.
Text fallback for unsupported browsers: included (FR4). Chosen because silently blocking Firefox/Safari users would make the reporter flow undemoable on those browsers.
Resolved/acknowledged alerts: collapse into a separate section rather than disappearing (FR8). Chosen so a grader or demo audience can review the full session's alerts without the main feed getting cluttered.
No /health endpoint: connectivity check reuses GET /api/stations instead (Section 5, step 1).
No acknowledged status: the operator's "Acknowledge" action sets status to monitoring, the closest existing enum value (Section 6, FR5).
No backend ETA: the dispatch card computes an estimate from distance_km at an assumed 40 km/h and labels it clearly as an estimate (Section 5, step 5).
Map tiles: standard OSM tile URL, no API key, client-side only (Section 6a).
11. Build sequence
Reporter page — SOS flow, voice capture (with text fallback, FR4), /api/alert integration with mock-mode, check-in loop (FR7), dispatch card.
Dashboard shell — Supabase Realtime connection (with mock-mode fallback), alert list, severity styling, critical-alert audio cue (no map yet).
Dashboard map — Leaflet integration per Section 6a, marker/list sync.
Alert lifecycle — status updates (PATCH /api/alerts/:id) wired to backend state, including the Acknowledge → monitoring mapping and the resolved-section collapse.
Polish pass — reduced-motion, focus states, empty/error states for both pages.